7Bv–æR¦öæRÂæ÷Bæ÷F†W"æÖRf÷"F†Rv†öÆRüâÚ$z{-®éÜj×urieu wine region is, how it fits within South Australiaâ€™s GI system, and why its maritime conditions support Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Riesling and more.","url":"/southern-fleurieu-wine-region/","text":"Highlights Southern Fleurieu is a protected Australian Geographical Indication (GI) in South Australia. It is a region within the Fleurieu wine zone, at the southern end of the Fleurieu Peninsula. Victor Harbor is the most useful regional centre for finding your bearings; Adelaide is roughly 1â€“1Â½ hours away by road, depending on the route and destination. The region has a maritime Mediterranean climate, with ocean influence, gentle hills and varied elevations. Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Riesling are the varieties highlighted in the regional profile. Shiraz, Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc are also part of the regionâ€™s documented plantings. â€œSouthern Fleurieuâ€ on a label indicates geographic origin. It does not guarantee one grape variety or one particular flavour profile. Quick answer Southern Fleurieu is a South Australian wine region at the southern end of the Fleurieu Peninsula. In Australiaâ€™s formal wine-geographical system, it is a protected GI classified as a region within the Fleurieu wine zone . The region is influenced by surrounding water and includes coastal and inland vineyard sites, from near sea level to higher areas in the ranges. Wine Australia describes its clim"},{"title":"Southern Highlands wine region: a guide to its wines, climate and place","description":"Learn where the Southern Highlands wine region is, how its cool elevated climate shapes wine, and what to expect from Chardonnay, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc and Cabernet Sauvignon.","url":"/southern-highlands-wine-region/","text":"The Southern Highlands is a protected Australian wine region in New South Wales. It sits within the South Coast wine zone and is best understood as an elevated, cool-climate region around Bowral, Mittagong, Moss Vale and Berrima. The region is known for fresh, often aromatic wines made from varieties including Chardonnay, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc and Cabernet Sauvignon. Pinot Noir and other varieties also contribute to its developing identity. However, there is no single â€œSouthern Highlands styleâ€: elevation, frost exposure, rainfall, soil, vintage and winemaking choices can all change the result. Highlights Southern Highlands is the official protected wine GI name. It is a region within the South Coast wine zone of New South Wales. The GI was entered in Australiaâ€™s Register of Protected Names on 25 March 2002. Bowral, Mittagong, Moss Vale and Berrima are useful places for understanding where the region is, but they are not interchangeable with the legal GI. Cool, moist and moderately continental conditions help explain the regionâ€™s freshness and vintage variation. Chardonnay, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc and Cabernet Sauvignon are the principal documented varieties. A Southern Highl"},{"title":"Strathbogie Ranges wine region: a beginnerâ€™s guide","description":"Understand where the Strathbogie Ranges wine region is, how its elevation and climate shape wine, and what to expect from Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc and Shiraz.","url":"/strathbogie-ranges-wine-region/","text":"Highlights Strathbogie Ranges is a protected wine Geographical Indication (GI) in central-north Victoria. It sits within the Central Victoria wine zone, alongside regions such as Goulburn Valley, Heathcote, Bendigo and Upper Goulburn. Euroa is the most useful town for understanding where the region is, although the town does not define the legal GI. The region ranges from lower, warmer country to high, cooler sites, creating noticeable variation between vineyards. Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc and Shiraz are the principal documented varieties. A Strathbogie Ranges label tells you about geographic originâ€”not a guaranteed grape variety, quality level or flavour. The region is roughly two hours north-east of Melbourne by road, depending on your route and destination. Quick answer: what is the Strathbogie Ranges wine region? Strathbogie Ranges is a protected Australian wine GI in Victoria. In Australiaâ€™s wine-geography system, it is a region within the Central Victoria wine zone . The region is in central-north Victoria, broadly east of the Hume Freeway and between the Seymour and Benalla areas. Euroa is the most useful practical orientation town, while Melbourne is the relev"},{"title":"Sunbury wine region: a guide to its place, wines and style","description":"Understand the Sunbury wine region in Victoria: its Port Phillip GI status, location north-west of Melbourne, climate, history, grape varieties and likely wine styles.","url":"/sunbury-wine-region/","text":"Highlights Sunbury is a protected Australian wine region in Victoriaâ€™s Port Phillip wine zone. It sits north-west of Melbourne, centred on the Sunbury district. The region is cool and dry, with wind, elevation and varied landforms shaping vineyard conditions. Shiraz is Sunburyâ€™s strongest traditional identity, often showing pepper, spice, dark fruit and fine tannins. Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir, Riesling and Viognier are also associated with the region. â€œSunburyâ€ on a wine label tells you about geographic origin. It does not guarantee a grape variety, flavour, quality or winemaking method. Sunbury is a separate GI from nearby Macedon Ranges, Geelong and Yarra Valley. Quick answer: what is the Sunbury wine region? Sunbury is a protected wine Geographical Indication (GI) in Victoria, Australia. It is classified as a region within the broader Port Phillip wine zone . The hierarchy is: Australia â†’ Victoria â†’ Port Phillip wine zone â†’ Sunbury region Sunbury is centred on the township of Sunbury, north-west of Melbourne. It is approximately 30â€“40 minutes from Melbourne city centre by road, depending on traffic, route and your destination. That travel time is"},{"title":"Swan District wine region: where it is, grapes and wine styles","description":"Learn where the Swan District wine region is, how it relates to Swan Valley and Greater Perth, and what to expect from its climate, grapes and wine styles.","url":"/swan-district-wine-region/","text":"The Swan District is a protected Australian wine region in Western Australia, immediately north of Perth. It sits within the Greater Perth wine zone and includes the better-known Swan Valley subregion . It is a warm, dry-ripening region with a long history of viticulture. Chenin Blanc, Verdelho, Chardonnay, Shiraz and fortified wines are among its important regional associations, but a Swan District label does not guarantee a particular grape, sweetness level or wine style. Highlights The Swan District is a formal Australian Geographical Indication (GI), not simply a tourism name. It is in the Greater Perth wine zone, north of metropolitan Perth. Swan Valley is a distinct subregion within the broader Swan District. The region has hot, dry summers, wetter winters and springs, and cooling afternoon sea breezes. Chenin Blanc and Verdelho are especially associated with the district, alongside Chardonnay, Shiraz and Cabernet Sauvignon. Fortified wines, including Muscat and Pedro XimÃ©nez styles, are part of its historical identity. A GI tells you about geographic originâ€”not necessarily how the wine will taste or where it was made. Where is the Swan District? The Swan District is in south"},{"title":"Swan Hill wine region: location, wines and what to expect","description":"Learn where the Swan Hill wine region is, how its cross-border GI differs from Murray terminology, and what to expect from its Chardonnay, Shiraz and Cabernet Sauvignon.","url":"/swan-hill-wine-region/","text":"Swan Hill is a protected Australian wine region, or Geographical Indication (GI), in the inland Murray area. It crosses the Murray River, covering parts of northern Victoria and southern New South Wales. The region is hot, dry and strongly shaped by its inland climate. Its principal documented grape varieties are Chardonnay, Shiraz and Cabernet Sauvignon. In broad terms, Swan Hill wines are often associated with fresh, approachable Chardonnay, smooth and fruity Shiraz, and berry-scented Cabernet Sauvignonâ€”although a regional name never guarantees one particular style. Highlights Location: Inland Murray area, between Mildura and Echuca States: Victoria and New South Wales Protected regional name: Swan Hill GI registered: 11 June 1996 Climate: Hot, dry and continental, with hot days and relatively cool nights Growing-season rainfall: About 177 mm Altitude: Approximately 47â€“129 metres Main documented varieties: Chardonnay, Shiraz and Cabernet Sauvignon Nearest useful regional centre: Swan Hill From Melbourne: The Swan Hill district is roughly a four-hour drive north-west, depending on the route and destination Where is the Swan Hill wine region? Swan Hill is centred around the Murray "},{"title":"Syrah vs Shiraz: Are They the Same Grape?","description":"Syrah and Shiraz are generally names for the same grape. Learn why the names differ, what Australian labels mean and how climate and winemaking shape the style.","url":"/syrah-vs-shiraz/","text":"Highlights Syrah and Shiraz are generally two names for the same red-wine grape variety. â€œSyrahâ€ is especially associated with France and the northern RhÃ´ne. â€œShirazâ€ is the established name commonly used in Australia. The name on the bottle can hint at a wine tradition, but it does not guarantee a particular flavour. Climate, region, ripeness, winemaking, oak and bottle age can all change the style. Australian Shiraz can be fresh and savoury, as well as rich and full-bodied. Quick answer Syrah and Shiraz are two widely used names for the same dark-skinned Vitis vinifera grape variety. The name Syrah is strongly associated with France, particularly the northern RhÃ´ne. Shiraz is the familiar Australian name for the variety and is also used in other wine-producing countries. The difference is therefore mainly about naming tradition and wine cultureâ€”not two separate grapes. A bottle labelled Shiraz in Australia normally refers to the same grape that may be labelled Syrah elsewhere. The names can offer clues about a wineâ€™s regional tradition or intended style. However, they do not work like a strict flavour code. A Syrah may be rich and powerful, while a Shiraz may be fresh, savoury an"},{"title":"Tasmania Wine Region: What Itâ€™s Like and What Wines Itâ€™s Known For","description":"Explore Tasmaniaâ€™s cool maritime wine region, its growing areas and the sparkling wine, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and Riesling it is known for.","url":"/tasmania-wine-region/","text":"Highlights Tasmania is a recognised Australian wine Geographical Indication covering the island state. It is a cool, maritime wine region with vineyards spread across northern, southern, eastern and north-western areas. Tasmania is particularly associated with Traditional Method sparkling wine, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Gris/Pinot Grigio are also important varieties. Tasmania is one broad GI, not seven legally separate wine subregions. Climate, elevation, site, variety, vintage, producer and winemaking can all change the style of a Tasmanian wine. A â€œTasmaniaâ€ label tells you about geographic origin, not a guaranteed grape variety, flavour or production method. Quick answer: what is Tasmaniaâ€™s wine region like? Tasmania is a cool, maritime Australian wine region known especially for sparkling wine, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. Its vineyards are spread across a sizeable island, from the Tamar Valley and Pipers River in the north to the Coal River Valley, Derwent Valley and Huon Valley in the south, with important growing areas along the east coast and in the north-west. The islandâ€™s surrounding seas, latitude, elevation and varied landscapes create"},{"title":"Tumbarumba Wine Region: A Guide to Its Place, Climate and Wines","description":"Understand the Tumbarumba wine region in southern New South Wales, including its GI, high-country climate, principal varieties and sparkling and still wine styles.","url":"/tumbarumba-wine-region/","text":"Highlights Tumbarumba is a protected Australian wine Geographical Indication (GI) in southern New South Wales. It sits within the Southern New South Wales wine zone, around the western foothills of the Snowy Mountains. The GI is broader than the town of Tumbarumba and reaches towards places including Tooma and Jingellic. Cool conditions, high elevation, cold nights and frost risk shape the regionâ€™s viticulture. Chardonnay and Pinot Noir are Tumbarumbaâ€™s principal varieties, with strong associations with sparkling wine. Still wines can be fresh, delicate and highly aromatic, although site, vintage and winemaking choices create variation. â€œTumbarumbaâ€ on a label indicates geographic origin. It does not guarantee one flavour profile or a particular level of quality. Quick answer: What is Tumbarumba known for? Tumbarumba is a cool-climate wine region in southern New South Wales, within the Southern New South Wales wine zone. It lies near the western foothills of the Snowy Mountains, close to the Murray River and the Victorian border. The region is best known for Chardonnay and Pinot Noir. These varieties are used especially for sparkling wine, where their natural acidity and freshness "},{"title":"Upper Goulburn Wine Region: A Beginnerâ€™s Guide","description":"Discover where Upper Goulburn is, how its elevation and terrain shape the region, and what to expect from its Chardonnay, Riesling and Sauvignon Blanc.","url":"/upper-goulburn-wine-region/","text":"Upper Goulburn is a cool, elevated wine region in north-eastern Victoria. It is particularly associated with Chardonnay, Riesling and Sauvignon Blanc, with elevation and hilly terrain helping create wines with freshness, fragrance and, in some examples, considerable weight. Highlights Upper Goulburn is a protected Australian wine Geographical Indication (GI) in Victoria. It sits within the Central Victoria GI framework but is a distinct regional indication in its own right. The region is commonly oriented around Yea, Alexandra, Eildon and Mansfield, although these towns should not be treated as interchangeable with the formal GI boundary. Vineyard sites are often discussed at about 300â€“700 metres, while the full GI has a much broader recorded altitude range. Chardonnay is the foremost variety identified by Wine Australia, alongside Riesling and Sauvignon Blanc. A wine using Upper Goulburn as a single GI claim must contain at least 85% grapes grown within the GI. Where is Upper Goulburn? Upper Goulburn is in Victoria, north-east of Melbourne, in the broader Victorian High Country landscape. Alexandra is a useful regional reference point for the southern and central part of the area,"},{"title":"What Are Tannins in Wine?","description":"Tannins are natural wine compounds that create grip and drying texture. Learn where they come from, how they feel and how they differ from acidity, sweetness and bitterness.","url":"/what-are-tannins/","text":"Highlights Tannins are naturally occurring compounds found mainly in grape skins, seeds and stems. Oak can contribute tannin too. Tannin feels more like texture than taste: drying, gripping, firm, rough or puckering. Tannin is not the same as sweetness, acidity, alcohol, bitterness or body. Red wines generally have more noticeable tannin because their juice spends more time with grape skins and other solids. White and rosÃ© wines can contain tannin, especially when made with skin contact, seed contact or oak. Tannin can add structure and contribute to ageing potential, but more tannin does not automatically mean better wine. Australian Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon and Pinot Noir can all show tannin, but the style varies from wine to wine. Quick answer: what are tannins? Tannins are a group of naturally occurring phenolic compounds. In grapes, they are found mainly in the skins and seeds, with stems also contributing when they are included during winemaking. Oak can add another source of tannin as wine matures in barrels or with other oak products. When you drink a tannic wine, you may notice that your gums and the inside of your cheeks feel dry, grippy or slightly rough. This sensatio"},{"title":"What Are Wine Grape Clones?","description":"Wine grape clones are selected vine lines within a grape variety. Learn how clones differ from varieties and rootstocks, why growers use them and what they canâ€”and cannotâ€”tell you about wine style.","url":"/what-are-wine-grape-clones/","text":"Highlights A grapevine clone is a selected vine reproduced through cuttings, buds or grafting. Clones belong to grape varieties such as Pinot Noir, Chardonnay or Shiraz. A clone is not the same as a rootstock: the clone provides the fruiting variety, while the rootstock provides the roots. Different clones may vary in ripening time, yield, bunch shape, berry size, vigour and health status. Clone choice can influence wine style, but it never determines the finished wine by itself. A clone number is not a guaranteed tasting note, quality score or prestige marker. Quick Answer: What Is a Wine Grape Clone? A wine grape clone is a group of grapevines descended through vegetative propagation from one selected parent vine. Instead of growing from seed, a vine is reproduced from a bud, cutting or grafted piece of the original plant. This preserves the selected vineâ€™s genetic identity with a high degree of similarity. The selected vine may have useful characteristics, such as a particular ripening time, yield, bunch structure, berry size or level of vigour. Once that material has been evaluated and multiplied, it may be identified as a clone within a grape variety. For example, Pinot Noir i"},{"title":"What Does Oak Do to Wine?","description":"Learn how oak can influence wine flavour, aroma, texture and ageing, and why not every oaked wine tastes the same.","url":"/what-does-oak-do-to-wine/","text":"Highlights Oak can change wine through wood-derived flavour and through the way wine matures in an oak vessel. Possible oak characters include vanilla, spice, toast, smoke, cedar and coconut. Oak can also affect texture, tannin and perceived body. New, small barrels usually make a more obvious impression than older, larger or neutral vessels. â€œOakedâ€ does not automatically mean sweeter, better or more expensive. Barrel fermentation and barrel maturation are related, but they are not the same thing. Australian Chardonnay, Shiraz and Cabernet Sauvignon can all be made with oakâ€”or without it. Quick Answer: How does oak affect wine? Oak affects wine in two main ways. First, wine extracts compounds from the wood. These can contribute aromas and flavours such as vanilla, spice, toast, smoke, char, cedar, coconut or caramel. Oak also contributes tannins that can affect bitterness, astringency and mouthfeel. Second, an oak barrel provides a particular environment for maturation. Because wood is porous, the wine can have limited, gradual interaction with oxygen. This can change colour, aroma, tannin structure and texture over time. Small amounts of oxygen may support some maturation reactio"},{"title":"What Does Yeast Do in Wine?","description":"Yeast turns grape sugar into alcohol, carbon dioxide and heat during fermentation. Learn how yeast affects wine aroma, texture and style.","url":"/what-does-yeast-do-in-wine/","text":"Highlights Yeast drives alcoholic fermentation. It consumes grape sugars and produces alcohol, carbon dioxide and heat. Yeast also creates compounds that can influence a wineâ€™s aroma, flavour and texture. Saccharomyces cerevisiae is commonly the main yeast carrying fermentation through to completion. Cultured yeast and ambient fermentation are different winemaking approachesâ€”not automatically better or worse. Yeast fermentation is different from malolactic fermentation, which is performed by bacteria. The finished wine reflects yeast, grapes, vintage and winemaking conditions working together. Quick Answer Yeast turns grape sugar into alcohol during alcoholic fermentation. It also produces carbon dioxide, heat and many other compounds that can influence the finished wineâ€™s aroma, flavour and texture. A simple way to think about it is: Grape sugar + yeast activity â†’ alcohol + carbon dioxide + heat + other flavour-active compounds The process is more than a chemical conversion. As yeast works, it changes the grape juice into wine. The type of yeast, the fermentation conditions and what happens afterwards can all affect the result. Yeast does not create a wineâ€™s entire character by it"},{"title":"What Is Acidity in Wine? How It Affects Taste","description":"Learn what acidity in wine tastes like, how it differs from pH, sweetness and tannin, and how variety, climate and winemaking shape freshness and balance.","url":"/what-is-acidity-in-wine/","text":"Highlights Acidity gives wine freshness, liveliness and a mouth-watering sensation. You may notice it as tartness, similar to the effect of citrus or green apple. Acidity is not the same as sweetness, tannin, sourness or pH. A high-acid wine is not automatically better, and a low-acid wine is not automatically faulty. Grape variety, ripeness, climate, harvest timing, winemaking and ageing can all influence acidity. You can recognise acidity by noticing how much your mouth waters and how fresh the wine feels. Quick Answer Acidity in wine is the presence and effect of naturally occurring acids that make wine feel fresh, lively and mouth-watering. It is one of wineâ€™s main structural components, working alongside sweetness, alcohol, tannin, fruit flavour and body. When acidity is noticeable, a wine may taste crisp, bright or energetic. It can create a tart sensation, rather like biting into a green apple or tasting citrus. A wine with less noticeable acidity may feel softer, rounder or broader. Neither style is automatically better. Acidity needs to make sense in the wine as a whole. It can keep a sweet wine from seeming heavy, give a rich white wine freshness, or help a red wine feel "},{"title":"What Is Balance in Wine? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide","description":"Learn what balance means in wine, how acidity, sweetness, alcohol, tannin, fruit and texture work together, and how to recognise balance when tasting.","url":"/what-is-balance-in-wine/","text":"Highlights Balance is the relationship between a wineâ€™s main elements, including acidity, sweetness, alcohol, tannin, fruit, body and texture. A balanced wine does not need every element in equal amounts. Balance does not mean a wine is light, mild or low in flavour. A powerful wine can still be balanced. The right balance depends on the wineâ€™s style. Sweet, sparkling, red, white and fortified wines are not judged by exactly the same expectations. You can recognise balance by noticing whether the wine feels coherent or whether one featureâ€”such as sharp acidity, hot alcohol or drying tanninâ€”takes over. Balance and personal preference are different. You can recognise that a wine is balanced without liking its style. Quick answer: what does â€œbalanced wineâ€ mean? A balanced wine is one in which its main elements work together so that the wine feels harmonious and proportionate for its style. No single feature distractingly dominates the experience. Those elements may include acidity, sweetness, alcohol, tannin, fruit concentration, body, texture, oak, bitterness and finish. They do not need to be equal, and balance is not a numerical formula. It is an overall tasting impression. For ex"},{"title":"What Is Biodynamic Wine?","description":"Learn how biodynamic wine relates to organic farming, certification and cellar practices, and what the label can and cannot tell you.","url":"/what-is-biodynamic-wine/","text":"Front matter Title: What Is Biodynamic Wine? Primary topic: Biodynamic wine and biodynamic viticulture Suggested description: Biodynamic wine comes from grapes grown using a farming system that builds on organic principles. Learn what biodynamic means, how it differs from organic and natural wine, and how to interpret the claim on an Australian wine label. Related live pages: What is wine? , How wine is made , Winemaking What Is Biodynamic Wine? Highlights Biodynamic wine generally comes from grapes grown using biodynamic vineyard practices. Biodynamic farming includes organic principles, plus additional preparations and whole-farm practices. Biodynamic describes how grapes are grownâ€”not one guaranteed flavour or wine style. Biodynamic wine is not automatically natural, vegan, additive-free, low-sulphur or healthier. Some biodynamic farms use lunar or cosmic calendars, but these ideas should not be confused with established scientific effects. In Australia, a producerâ€™s biodynamic claim and an independent certification mark are not necessarily the same thing. Quick answer Biodynamic wine is wine made from grapes grown using biodynamic viticulture : a farming system that incorporate"},{"title":"What Is Brettanomyces in Wine?","description":"Brettanomyces, or â€œBrettâ€, is a yeast that can change wineâ€™s aroma and flavour. Learn what Brett smells like, when it is a fault and how it differs from other wine faults.","url":"/what-is-brettanomyces/","text":"Highlights Brettanomyces is a yeast associated with wine spoilage. It is often shortened to â€œBrettâ€. Brett can change a wineâ€™s smell and flavour, sometimes producing medicinal, smoky, leathery, earthy or barnyard-like notes. A single aroma descriptor does not prove that Brettanomyces is present. Brett can suppress fresh fruit and make a wine seem muted or less expressive. Some drinkers tolerate low levels of Brett character, but higher levels are commonly considered a fault. Brett is different from cork taint, oxidation, reduction and volatile acidity. Consumers usually cannot reverse established Brett character at home. Quick answer Brettanomyces is a wine-associated yeast, particularly the species Brettanomyces bruxellensis , that can grow during winemaking, maturation or storage. It can produce volatile compounds that alter a wineâ€™s aroma and flavour. Depending on the wine and the level present, Brett character may smell medicinal, like a bandage or antiseptic; smoky or spicy; leathery and earthy; or animal-like, sometimes described as â€œbarnyardâ€, â€œhorseâ€ or â€œstableâ€. It may also do something less obvious: suppress the wineâ€™s fruit, making it seem dull, muted or less fresh. Is B"},{"title":"What Is Cabernet Sauvignon?","description":"Cabernet Sauvignon is a structured red grape known for dark fruit, acidity and firm tannin. Learn how its style varies and where it thrives in Australia.","url":"/what-is-cabernet-sauvignon/","text":"Highlights Cabernet Sauvignon is a red wine grape variety. The name is also used for wines made mainly from that grape. It commonly produces deeply coloured, medium- to full-bodied wines. Typical Cabernet Sauvignon has noticeable acidity, firm tannin and dark-fruit flavours. Blackcurrant, blackberry, mulberry and plum are common descriptors, but every bottle is different. Climate, ripeness, oak, blending and bottle age can change the wine considerably. Most still Cabernet Sauvignon is dry, even when it tastes richly fruity. Australian examples range from elegant, cool-climate wines to fuller, more structured styles. Some Cabernet Sauvignon can age for many years, but plenty is made to enjoy now. Quick answer: what is Cabernet Sauvignon? Cabernet Sauvignon is a red wine grape variety . It is one of the worldâ€™s best-known grapes and is used to make both single-variety wines and blends. Cabernet Sauvignon wines are often medium to full bodied, with deep colour, noticeable acidity and firm or structured tannins. Tannin is the part of wine that can make your mouth feel dry or grippy. The grape commonly produces dark-fruit flavours such as blackcurrant, blackberry, mulberry and plum. Tha"},{"title":"What Is Chardonnay? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to Its Flavours and Styles","description":"Chardonnay is a versatile white wine grape that can make fresh, citrusy, oaky, creamy, still or sparkling wines. Learn what it tastes like and why styles vary.","url":"/what-is-chardonnay/","text":"Highlights Chardonnay is a white wine grape variety, not one fixed style of wine. It can taste fresh and citrusy, or rich, textured and creamy. Most still Chardonnay is dry, even when it smells fruity or buttery. Climate, ripeness, vineyard site and winemaking all shape its character. Oak can add toast, spice and vanilla, but Chardonnay is not automatically oaky. Malolactic fermentation can soften acidity and contribute buttery aromas. Australia produces Chardonnay in regions ranging from Tasmania and Adelaide Hills to Margaret River and Victoria. Chablis is a French region whose wines are made from Chardonnayâ€”not a different grape. Quick Answer: What Is Chardonnay? Chardonnay is a white wine grape variety. The name also commonly refers to wine made from that grape. One reason Chardonnay is so popular is that it can make many different styles. A cool-climate Chardonnay may be light to medium-bodied, fresh and citrus-driven, with flavours such as lemon, green apple or white peach. A riper or more richly made example may be fuller and rounder, with peach, pear, melon or tropical fruit. Oak maturation, lees contact and malolactic fermentation can add texture and flavours such as toast"},{"title":"What Is Cold Soak in Winemaking?","description":"Cold soak is a cool, pre-fermentation period of skin contact used mainly in red-wine production. Learn how it may influence colour, aroma, flavour and tannin.","url":"/what-is-cold-soak/","text":"Highlights Cold soak is a cool period of skin contact before alcoholic fermentation begins. It is mainly used when making red wine. Winemakers use it to influence colour, aroma, flavour and texture. Cold soak may delay or limit fermentation, but it does not necessarily stop all microbial activity. The result depends on the grapes, temperature, duration and what happens afterwards. It is not the same as cold fermentation, cold stabilisation or carbonic maceration. Cold soak is a winemaking choiceâ€”not a guarantee of a darker, fruitier, smoother or better wine. Quick answer Cold soak is a pre-fermentation period when crushed red grapes or grape must are kept cool while the juice remains in contact with the skins and seeds . The cool conditions are intended to delay or limit alcoholic fermentation while extraction begins. Winemakers may use cold soak to influence the wineâ€™s colour, aroma, flavour and tannin structure. Before alcohol is produced, the extraction environment is different from the one created during fermentation. This can change which compounds move from the skins and seeds into the juice. The effect is not automatic. Cold soak may contribute to deeper colour or more notic"},{"title":"What Is Complexity in Wine?","description":"Wine complexity explained in plain English: learn how to recognise layers, development and integration, and how complexity differs from intensity, oak, age and quality.","url":"/what-is-complexity-in-wine/","text":"Highlights Complexity is the impression that a wine offers several distinct, connected and sometimes evolving aromas, flavours and textures. It is not simply a long list of tasting-note descriptors. A wine can be intense but simple, or restrained but complex. Age, oak, price and concentration may contribute to complexity, but none guarantees it. Complexity is different from balance, length, quality and personal preference. You can look for complexity by smelling, tasting and revisiting a wine slowly. Quick Answer: What Does Complexity Mean in Wine? Complexity in wine describes the range, interaction and development of the sensations a wine offers. A complex wine may show fruit, floral, herbal, spicy, savoury, earthy or developed characteristics, with these details appearing together or revealing themselves over time. The key is not how many flavours you can name. Complexity is more about whether the wine offers several recognisable dimensions that feel connected and continue to interest you. One sip might begin with dark fruit, then reveal spice, savoury notes and a fine, lingering texture. Complexity is a sensory impression, not a direct measurement of how many chemical compounds "},{"title":"What Is Cork Taint?","description":"Cork taint can mute fruit and create musty aromas. Learn how to recognise it, what causes it and what to do with an affected bottle.","url":"/what-is-cork-taint/","text":"Highlights Cork taint is a wine fault usually associated with a compound called TCA . It can smell like damp cardboard, wet newspaper, a mouldy cellar or a musty basement. The wineâ€™s normal fruit may seem muted, flat or strangely quiet. Cork taint is not the same as cork fragments, sediment, oxidation or every unpleasant smell. It usually makes wine defective and unpleasant rather than enjoyable to drink as intended. Natural cork is a common pathway, but contamination can also come from winery materials or environments. If a bottle seems cork-tainted, keep the bottle, closure and proof of purchase where practical, then contact the retailer. Quick answer: what is cork taint? Cork taint is an unpleasant sensory fault in wine, most commonly associated with 2,4,6-trichloroanisole , usually shortened to TCA . Affected wine may smell musty or mouldy, like damp cardboard, wet newspaper or a dusty cellar. Its usual fruit aromas can seem suppressed, leaving the wine dull, flat or lifeless. The name can be misleading. Cork taint does not necessarily mean the cork looks damaged, and it does not prove that the closure alone caused the problem. TCA and related compounds can be associated with n"},{"title":"What Is Fermentation in Wine?","description":"Fermentation is the process in which yeast converts grape sugar into alcohol, carbon dioxide and flavour compounds. Learn how it shapes wine.","url":"/what-is-fermentation/","text":"Highlights Fermentation is the biological process that turns grape sugar into alcohol and carbon dioxide. Yeast is the main microorganism responsible for alcoholic fermentation. Fermentation also produces heat and compounds that influence aroma and flavour. Fermentation is one stage of winemakingâ€”not a name for every cellar operation. Red, white and rosÃ© wines can ferment in different ways, especially in their contact with grape skins. Malolactic fermentation is a separate bacterial process that changes acidity; it does not create the wineâ€™s alcohol. Australian wineries manage fermentation differently depending on climate, grape variety, vintage and desired style. Quick Answer: What is fermentation? In winemaking, fermentation is a biological process in which microorganisms convert grape sugars into new compounds. During alcoholic fermentation , yeast consumes fermentable sugars and produces ethanol, carbon dioxide and heat. A simple way to picture it is: grape sugars â†’ alcohol + carbon dioxide + heat That equation is useful, but incomplete. Yeast also produces other compounds that can contribute to a wineâ€™s aroma, flavour and texture. The way fermentation proceeds can influence wh"},{"title":"What Is Fortified Wine? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide","description":"Fortified wine is wine with distilled spirit added. Learn why some styles are sweet, others dry and what Australian fortified terms mean.","url":"/what-is-fortified-wine/","text":"Highlights Fortified wine is wine with distilled spirit added to increase its alcohol level. The spirit may be added before fermentation finishes or after it has finished. Early fortification usually leaves more natural grape sugar, creating a sweeter wine. Later fortification can produce a dry fortified wine. Fortified wine is not the same thing as sweet wine, dessert wine or a spirit. Fortified wines can be shaped by wood, oxygen, flor yeast and bottle maturation. Australia has a long fortified-wine history, particularly with styles from Rutherglen. Apera and Topaque are the current Australian terms associated with wines once commonly called Australian Sherry and Tokay. Quick answer Fortified wine is wine that has had distilled spirit added to it, increasing its alcoholic strength. The base wine is made through fermentation, like other wine, but the added spirit changes its alcohol level, texture, sweetness and potential for ageing. The timing of that addition matters. If spirit is added while fermentation is still under way, it can stop or inhibit the yeast. Some natural grape sugar remains, so the finished wine is often sweet. If the wine is allowed to ferment until it is dry a"},{"title":"What Is Grenache?","description":"Grenache is a versatile red grape known for red fruit, spice and warmth. Learn about its Australian styles, blends and growing regions.","url":"/what-is-grenache/","text":"Highlights Grenache is a red wine grape variety, also commonly called Garnacha in Spain. It can make red wine, rosÃ©, fortified wine and sparkling wine, although red Grenache is the main focus here. Typical red Grenache may show strawberry, raspberry, plum, spice, white pepper, herbs or savoury notes. It is often medium-bodied and generous in alcohol, but can range from light and fresh to rich and structured. Grenache may be made as a varietal wine or blended with grapes such as Shiraz and MourvÃ¨dre. In Australia, it has an especially strong connection with South Australia, including McLaren Vale and the Barossa Valley. Fruity Grenache is not necessarily sweet: most still red Grenache is dry. Quick answer: what is Grenache? Grenache is a red wine grape variety used to make wines in many styles. It is known for ripe berry flavours, fragrant aromas and the ability to produce generous alcohol, but it does not have one fixed personality. A lighter Grenache might taste bright, juicy and fresh, with strawberry or raspberry flavours. A richer example might feel warm, full and concentrated, with plum, spice, earth or savoury notes. Tannin and acidity also vary, so Grenache can be supple and"},{"title":"What Is Malolactic Fermentation?","description":"Malolactic fermentation is a bacterial conversion that changes malic acid into lactic acid. Learn how it affects acidity, texture and aroma, why winemakers manage it, and why it matters in Australian red wine and Chardonnay.","url":"/what-is-malolactic-fermentation/","text":"Highlights Malolactic fermentation (MLF) is a bacterial conversion that changes malic acid into lactic acid and carbon dioxide. It is different from alcoholic fermentation, which is driven mainly by yeast and produces alcohol. MLF can make acidity seem less sharp and may give wine a rounder texture. It can sometimes contribute buttery or creamy aromas, but it does not automatically make wine buttery. MLF is common in many red wines and is used selectively in white wines, including Australian Chardonnay. Winemakers may encourage, prevent or partly manage MLF depending on the style they want. Quick answer Malolactic fermentation is a stage of winemaking in which lactic-acid bacteria convert malic acid into lactic acid and carbon dioxide. Malic acid is a naturally occurring grape acid. It can contribute to a sharper, more angular impression in wine. Lactic acid is generally perceived as softer, so MLF usually reduces the sense of sharpness and changes the wineâ€™s acid balance. It does not remove all acidity, and it does not guarantee that a wine will taste soft or creamy. Despite its name, MLF is not the same as the alcoholic fermentation that produces wineâ€™s alcohol. Alcoholic ferment"},{"title":"What Is Merlot?","description":"Merlot is a red grape known for plum and dark-fruit flavours, moderate tannin and a smooth texture. Learn how its styles vary.","url":"/what-is-merlot/","text":"title: What Is Merlot? description: Merlot is a red wine grape variety known for its ripe fruit flavours and often supple texture. Learn what Merlot tastes like, whether it is sweet and how to choose a bottle. canonical: https://winedaddy.com.au/what-is-merlot/ What Is Merlot? Highlights Merlot is a red wine grape variety , as well as the name commonly used for wines made from it. Most still Merlot is dry , even when it tastes of ripe plum or blackberry. Merlot often has a soft, rounded texture, moderate acidity and relatively supple tannins. It can range from lighter and fresher to fuller, richer and more structured. Australian Merlot is made both as a single-variety wine and in blends, often with Cabernet Sauvignon. Merlot can suit everyday foods such as burgers, sausages, roast chicken, pizza and mushroom dishes. Quick answer: what is Merlot wine? Merlot is a red grape variety used to make red wine. A bottle labelled â€œMerlotâ€ is usually a wine made primarily from that grape, although Merlot is also used in blends. Merlot commonly produces wines with plum, blackberry, black cherry and other ripe-fruit flavours. Depending on where it is grown and how it is made, it may also show c"},{"title":"What Is Natural Wine?","description":"Natural wine usually means a low-intervention approach, but there is no single universal definition. Learn what the term does and does not tell you.","url":"/what-is-natural-wine/","text":"Highlights Natural wine usually means wine made with a low-intervention approach. There is no single, universally accepted definition of â€œnatural wineâ€ in Australia. Natural wine is not automatically organic, biodynamic, vegan, additive-free, unfiltered or sulphur-free. Native yeast, limited additions, minimal filtration and little or no added sulphur are common associations, not compulsory rules. Cloudiness, sediment and unusual aromas can occur, but they are not guarantees of quality or authenticity. The best way to understand a bottle is to look beyond the front label and check the producerâ€™s notes. Quick answer: what is natural wine? Natural wine is generally made with a low-intervention approach. That often means the winemaker aims to let the grapes and fermentation speak for themselves, using fewer additions, adjustments and processing steps than many conventionally made wines. The difficulty is that natural wine does not have one fixed definition or universal production checklist . One producerâ€™s natural wine might be made with organically farmed grapes, native yeast and no filtration. Another might use some different combination of farming or cellar practices. The word itse"},{"title":"What Is Nebbiolo?","description":"Nebbiolo is a pale-coloured but structured red grape known for high acidity, firm tannin and complex aromas. Learn how to approach it.","url":"/what-is-nebbiolo/","text":"Highlights Nebbiolo is a red-wine grape variety , not a region or a single wine style. It is strongly associated with Piedmont in north-western Italy. Barolo and Barbaresco are famous wines made from Nebbiolo. Nebbiolo can look relatively pale but still have pronounced acidity and firm tannins. Common flavour and aroma associations include red cherry, rose, violet, herbs, spice, earth and savoury notes. Most conventional still Nebbiolo is dry. Australian Nebbiolo can vary from powerful and structured to fresher, more approachable or rosÃ© styles. Quick answer: what is Nebbiolo? Nebbiolo is an Italian red-wine grape variety. It is best known as the grape used to make Barolo and Barbaresco, two important wines from Piedmont. But Nebbiolo is the grape; Barolo and Barbaresco are named wines made under particular regional rules. Nebbiolo wine is often a fascinating contrast. In the glass, it may look relatively pale or transparent compared with deeply coloured red varieties. In the mouth, however, it can feel energetic and substantial, with fresh acidity and firm tannin. Typical Nebbiolo aromas and flavours may include red cherry, raspberry, rose, violet, herbs, spice, earth and savoury "},{"title":"What Is Organic Wine?","description":"Learn what organic wine means in Australia, how certification works and why organic does not automatically mean natural, vegan or preservative-free.","url":"/what-is-organic-wine/","text":"Primary topic: Organic wine Reading time: About 7 minutes Highlights Organic wine begins with grapes grown under organic-farming requirements. Depending on the claim and standard, organic rules may also apply to winemaking. Organic wine is not automatically natural, biodynamic, vegan, additive-free or sulphite-free. Australian wine sold domestically does not have to be certified to carry an organic claim, but the claim must be truthful and supported. â€œCertified organicâ€ and â€œmade with organic grapesâ€ are not necessarily the same claim. Organic status does not create one particular flavour. Organic wine can be red, white, rosÃ© or sparkling, in many different styles. The label wording and any certification mark are more useful than assumptions about what â€œorganicâ€ must mean. Quick Answer: What Does Organic Wine Mean? Organic wine is wine made from grapes grown according to organic-farming requirements. Depending on the wording on the bottle and the applicable standard, organic requirements may also cover cellar practices such as permitted additives, processing aids, winemaking methods and labelling. In simple terms, organic wine is about how the grapes and, in some cases, the wine ar"},{"title":"What Is Oxidation in Wine?","description":"Learn how oxygen can help or harm wine, how oxidation tastes and smells, and when oxidative character is intentional rather than a fault.","url":"/what-is-oxidation-in-wine/","text":"Topic: Wine oxidation Highlights Oxidation happens when wine compounds react with oxygen over time. Unwanted oxidation can make wine seem dull, flat, tired or brown. Possible aromas include bruised apple, nuts, cardboard, straw or hay. Oxidation is not always a fault: some wines are deliberately made in an oxidative style. One smell or a change in colour does not prove that a wine is oxidised. Resealing an opened bottle, limiting air exposure and avoiding heat can slow further change. Chilling may slow oxidation, but it cannot reverse established chemical changes. Oxidation is different from cork taint, reduction and volatile acidity. Quick Answer Oxidation in wine is a group of chemical changes caused by wineâ€™s exposure to oxygen. Over time, oxygen can change a wineâ€™s colour, aroma, flavour and sense of freshness. Unwanted oxidation may mute fresh fruit and floral aromas, make the wine taste flat or tired, and produce notes such as bruised apple, nuts, cardboard, straw, hay or sherry. White wines may become noticeably darker or browner, while red wines can lose bright fruit and develop a browner, more developed appearance. But oxidation is not automatically a fault. Winemakers som"},{"title":"What Is Pinot Gris / Pinot Grigio? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide","description":"Pinot Gris and Pinot Grigio are names for the same grape. Learn why both appear on labels, how the styles can differ and what Australian drinkers should look for.","url":"/what-is-pinot-gris-pinot-grigio/","text":"Highlights Pinot Gris and Pinot Grigio are two names for the same white-wine grape variety . Pinot Gris is the French name; Pinot Grigio is the Italian name. The grape has greyish-pink or dusky skins, but it is normally made into white wine. Pinot Grigio is often associated with light, crisp and fresh wines. Pinot Gris is often associated with riper, fuller and more textured wines. Those style associations are tendencies, not rules. Australian producers may use either name for different styles. Climate, harvest timing and winemaking choices can matter more than the name on the front label. Quick Answer Pinot Gris and Pinot Grigio are names for the same grape variety. â€œPinot Grisâ€ is French, while â€œPinot Grigioâ€ is Italian. Both names refer to the grapeâ€™s greyish or dusky skin colour. The variety is usually made as a dry white wine. Depending on where it is grown and how it is made, it can be light, crisp and citrusy, or fuller, riper and more textured. Common flavours include apple, pear, citrus and stone fruit, with some wines showing mineral, spice, creamy or lightly savoury notes. The names can offer a useful clue to style. Pinot Grigio is often linked with earlier-picked, fresh"},{"title":"What Is Pinot Noir? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to the Red Wine","description":"Pinot Noir is a red grape variety known for fresh acidity, red-fruit flavours and often lighter to medium-bodied wines. Learn what it tastes like, whether it is sweet and where it is grown in Australia.","url":"/what-is-pinot-noir/","text":"Highlights Pinot Noir is a red grape variety and the name of the wine made from it. It commonly produces light- to medium-bodied red wines , although some examples are fuller and more concentrated. Typical flavours include cherry, strawberry, raspberry, floral, earthy and savoury notes . Pinot Noir usually has fresh acidity and fine to moderate tannin. Most still Pinot Noir is dry , even when it tastes strongly of ripe fruit. Australian Pinot Noir is especially associated with cooler regions , including Tasmania, the Yarra Valley, Mornington Peninsula and Adelaide Hills. Pinot Noir can be delicate and silky, but it is not automatically pale, weak, sweet or simple. Quick answer Pinot Noir is a dark-skinned wine grape variety used mainly to make red wine. The name â€œPinot Noirâ€ can refer to the grape itself or to a bottle of wine made primarily from that grape. Most Pinot Noir is red, dry and light to medium bodied. It often has noticeable acidity, relatively fine tannin and red-fruit flavours such as cherry, strawberry and raspberry. Depending on where it was grown and how it was made, it may also show floral, earthy, savoury or spicy notes. Pinot Noir is strongly associated with coo"},{"title":"What Is Reduction in Wine? Smells, Causes and Whether Air Helps","description":"Reduction in wine can smell like struck match, rubber or rotten egg. Learn what it means, when it may be stylistic, and whether opening the bottle can help.","url":"/what-is-reduction-in-wine/","text":"Highlights Reduction is a broad term for sulphur-related wine character, often associated with low-oxygen conditions. It may smell like struck match, flint, rubber, cabbage, onion or rotten egg. Not every reductive aroma is a fault. Subtle flinty notes may be part of a wineâ€™s style. Some temporary reductive aromas soften after opening and swirling. Persistent, unpleasant sulphur character may not improve with ordinary aeration. Reduction is different from oxidation, cork taint and Brettanomyces. One aroma cannot identify a specific chemical compound or winemaking cause. Quick answer: what does reduction mean in wine? Reduction in wine is a sensory and chemical condition associated mainly with volatile sulphur compounds and low-oxygen or â€œreductiveâ€ conditions during winemaking or storage. Depending on the compounds involved, concentration and wine style, reduction may smell of struck match, struck flint, rubber, cabbage, onion, sewage or rotten egg. Some sulphur-related aromas can add complexity or be tolerated in small amounts. Strong or persistent character, however, can overwhelm the wineâ€™s fruit and make it unpleasant. Can air improve it? Sometimes. Opening the bottle, swirling"},{"title":"What Is Riesling? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to This Aromatic White Wine","description":"Riesling is an aromatic white grape that can make dry, off-dry and sweet wines. Learn how acidity, fruitiness and Australian regions shape its style.","url":"/what-is-riesling/","text":"Highlights Riesling is a white grape variety, not a single sweetness level. It is often aromatic, light- to medium-bodied and high in acidity. Common flavours include lime, lemon, green apple, peach and floral notes. Riesling can be bone-dry, off-dry, sweet or occasionally sparkling. Most Australian Riesling is made in a crisp, dry style. Clare Valley and Eden Valley are important Australian examples, but Riesling is grown in many regions. Some Rieslings can age and develop honeyed, toasty or nutty flavours. Quick answer: what is Riesling? Riesling is a white wine grape variety. The name is also commonly used for wines made primarily from that grape. Riesling is best known for its lively acidity and fragrant character. A young Riesling may smell or taste of lime, lemon, green apple, peach or flowers. Those flavours can appear in a completely dry wine, so Riesling is not automatically sweet. The grape can produce several styles, from bone-dry to off-dry, sweet and dessert wines. In Australia, Riesling is especially associated with crisp, dry wines from regions such as Clare Valley and Eden Valley, although Tasmania, Great Southern, Canberra District and other regions also produce it"},{"title":"What Is RosÃ©? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to Pink Wine","description":"RosÃ© is a wine style, not a single grape or sweetness level. Learn how it gets its colour and how to choose, serve and enjoy it.","url":"/what-is-rose/","text":"Highlights RosÃ© is a wine style with a colour ranging from pale pink and salmon to deeper pink and copper. It is usually made from red grapes, but with much less skin contact than red wine. RosÃ© is not defined by one grape, region, sweetness level or production method. It can be dry, off-dry or sweet, and pale colour does not automatically mean dry wine. Direct pressing, short skin contact and saignÃ©e are three ways to make rosÃ©. RosÃ© is generally not made by mixing finished red and white wine. Australian rosÃ© is made from many grape varieties and regions. Serve it chilled, then reseal and refrigerate any leftovers. Quick answer RosÃ© is a wine style known for its pink-to-copper colour and fresh, fruit-driven character. It is most commonly made from red grapes, but the juice spends only limited time with the grape skins. That brief contact gives the wine some colour without the extended extraction associated with most red wine. RosÃ© is therefore not a single grape variety or a synonym for a sweet wine. A bottle can be dry, off-dry or sweet; pale or deeply coloured; light-bodied or more structured; still or sparkling. The colour usually comes from controlled contact between juice and"},{"title":"What Is Sangiovese?","description":"Sangiovese is an Italian red grape known for bright acidity, savoury character and red-fruit flavours. Learn what to expect in Australian examples.","url":"/what-is-sangiovese/","text":"title: What is Sangiovese? description: Sangiovese is an Italian red wine grape known for cherry flavours, lively acidity and noticeable tannin. Learn what Sangiovese wine tastes like and how styles vary. canonical: https://winedaddy.com.au/what-is-sangiovese/ article_type: Educational Guide What is Sangiovese? Highlights Sangiovese is an Italian red wine grape variety, not one single wine style. It is best known for dry red wines with cherry flavours, lively acidity and noticeable tannin. Plum, herbs, spice and savoury notes may also appear, depending on the wine. Chianti and Brunello di Montalcino are famous Sangiovese-based appellations, not definitions of every Sangiovese wine. Australian Sangiovese can be brighter, riper, darker or more savoury depending on its region and how it is made. Its acidity and tannin make it a natural partner for tomato-based dishes, pizza and grilled food. Quick answer Sangiovese is an Italian red wine grape variety strongly associated with Tuscany. It is used to make wines ranging from light and fresh to dark, firm and structured. A typical Sangiovese wine is dry, with flavours of cherry, sour cherry and other red fruits. It often has lively acidit"},{"title":"What Is Sauvignon Blanc? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to Its Flavour and Style","description":"Sauvignon Blanc is a fresh, aromatic white wine grape variety. Learn what it tastes like, whether it is sweet and how Australian styles vary.","url":"/what-is-sauvignon-blanc/","text":"Highlights Sauvignon Blanc is a white wine grape variety and the name commonly used for wines made from it. It is often fresh, aromatic and high in perceived acidity, with flavours such as citrus, grapefruit, passionfruit, herbs or grass. Most still Sauvignon Blanc is dry. Fruity or tropical flavours do not necessarily mean the wine contains much sugar. It is not always grassy, sweet, light-bodied or unoaked. Australian examples vary from crisp, aromatic wines to broader Sauvignon Blancâ€“Semillon blends. Serve it chilled and try it with seafood, salads, goatâ€™s cheese or fresh, herb-led dishes. Quick Answer: What Is Sauvignon Blanc? Sauvignon Blanc is a white wine grape variety. The same name is also commonly used for wines made primarily from that grape. It is best known for producing fresh, aromatic white wines with noticeable acidity. Depending on where the grapes are grown and how the wine is made, you might taste lemon, lime, grapefruit, passionfruit, guava, gooseberry, freshly cut grass, herbs or green capsicum. Most still Sauvignon Blanc is made in a dry style. That does not mean it lacks fruit flavour. A wine can taste strongly of passionfruit or tropical fruit while containi"},{"title":"What Is Shiraz? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to Australiaâ€™s Favourite Red Grape","description":"Shiraz is a versatile red wine grape known as Syrah internationally. Learn what Shiraz tastes like, whether it is sweet and how Australian regions shape its style.","url":"/what-is-shiraz/","text":"Highlights Shiraz is a dark-skinned grape used mainly to make red wine. Syrah is the principal international name for the same grape variety. Australian Shiraz can be medium-bodied and savoury, or rich, full-bodied and dark-fruited. Typical flavours may include blackberry, plum, spice, liquorice, pepper, floral notes and savoury characters. Shiraz is usually made as a dry wine, even when it tastes ripe, rich or jammy. Climate, vineyard site, ripeness and winemaking all help shape the final wine. Barossa Valley, McLaren Vale, Hunter Valley, Heathcote and cooler regions can produce noticeably different styles. Quick Answer Shiraz is a dark-skinned wine grape variety, usually made into red wine. In Australia, Shiraz is the familiar name used on labels and by wine drinkers. Internationally, the same grape is commonly called Syrah . There is no single Shiraz flavour or style. Depending on where the grapes grow and how the wine is made, Shiraz may be medium- to full-bodied, with flavours ranging from red and dark fruit to black pepper, spice, liquorice, flowers and savoury notes. It may feel fresh and peppery, plush and generous, or firm and structured. Australian Shiraz is especially di"},{"title":"What Is Sparkling Wine? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide","description":"Learn what creates the bubbles, how sparkling wine is made and why Champagne is one protected regional style.","url":"/what-is-sparkling-wine/","text":"Highlights Sparkling wine is wine containing dissolved carbon dioxideâ€”the gas that creates its bubbles. The bubbles can be created by fermentation in a bottle or tank, or added mechanically through carbonation. Sparkling wine can be white, rosÃ© or red, and range from very dry to sweet. Champagne is a protected regional name, not a generic word for sparkling wine. Traditional-method wines may develop bready or toasty flavours through ageing on yeast sediment. Australia makes sparkling wine in several regions and styles, including sparkling Shiraz. Quick answer Sparkling wine is wine with enough dissolved carbon dioxide to create visible bubbles and a lively, tingling texture when the bottle is opened and the wine is poured. The carbon dioxide can come from fermentation. In the traditional method, a second fermentation happens inside the bottle. In the tank method, it happens in a sealed pressure tank. Some sparkling wines are carbonated by adding carbon dioxide mechanically instead. This means sparkling wine is a broad category, not one single style. It may be white, rosÃ© or red; dry or sweet; light and fruit-driven or richer and more savoury. Champagne is one particular type of spa"},{"title":"What Is Sweet Wine? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide","description":"Learn what makes wine taste sweet, how residual sugar works and why fruity does not necessarily mean sweet.","url":"/what-is-sweet-wine/","text":"Highlights Sweet wine tastes sweet because it contains enough residual sugar for you to notice it. Residual sugar is grape sugar left in the wine after fermentation. A wine that smells fruity is not necessarily sweet. Acidity can balance sweetness and make a wine feel fresh rather than heavy. Alcohol warmth, tannin and body are different sensations from sweetness. Sweetness can range from a slight softness to an intensely sugary, concentrated taste. â€œSweetâ€, â€œoff-dryâ€ and â€œdessert wineâ€ do not always mean exactly the same thing everywhere. Sweet wine is a style characteristic, not a measure of quality. Quick answer Sweet wine is wine with noticeable sweetness, usually because some grape sugar remains after fermentation. This remaining sugar is called residual sugar . During fermentation, yeast converts grape sugar into alcohol and carbon dioxide. When fermentation continues until almost all fermentable sugar has been used, the wine is generally dry. If fermentation stops or finishes while some sugar remains, the wine can taste sweet. That is only part of the story. A wineâ€™s acidity, alcohol, tannin, aroma and serving temperature all influence how sweet or balanced it seems. A dry w"},{"title":"What Is Sweetness in Wine?","description":"Learn what sweetness means in wine, how residual sugar affects the palate, and why a fruity wine can still be technically dry.","url":"/what-is-sweetness-in-wine/","text":"Highlights Sweetness is the sweet sensation you notice on your palate. Residual sugarâ€”the grape sugar left after fermentationâ€”is its main direct source. A fruity-smelling wine can still be technically dry. â€œDryâ€ usually means little perceptible sweetness, not little flavour. Acidity, alcohol, tannin, bitterness, body, texture and temperature all affect how sweet a wine seems. Red, white, rosÃ©, sparkling and fortified wines can all be dry or sweet. Grape variety, colour and region are cluesâ€”not guaranteesâ€”of sweetness. Quick answer: what does sweetness mean in wine? Sweetness in wine is the perception of sugar on your palate. It mainly comes from residual sugar , which is grape sugar left in the wine after fermentation. During fermentation, yeast converts grape sugar into alcohol and carbon dioxide. When most of the sugar is fermented, the wine will generally taste dry. When a noticeable amount remains, the wine may taste off-dry, medium-sweet or sweet. But sweetness is not simply a sugar reading. Acidity can make a wine feel fresher and less heavy, while tannin or bitterness can make it seem drier. Alcohol may add warmth or roundness, and fruit aromas can make a wine smell sweet ev"},{"title":"What Is Tempranillo?","description":"Tempranillo is a Spanish red grape that can make juicy young wines or complex oak-aged styles. Learn what Australian drinkers should expect.","url":"/what-is-tempranillo/","text":"title: &quot;What Is Tempranillo? Taste, Style and Australian Examples&quot; description: &quot;Tempranillo is a Spanish red-wine grape known for dry wines with red and dark fruit, fresh acidity and noticeable tannin. Learn what it tastes like and how Australian examples can differ.&quot; canonical: &quot; https://winedaddy.com.au/what-is-tempranillo/ &quot; article_type: &quot;Educational Guide&quot; What Is Tempranillo? Highlights Tempranillo is a red-wine grape variety , not one fixed style of wine. It is strongly associated with Spain, especially Rioja and Ribera del Duero. Tempranillo wines are usually dry, with red and dark fruit, fresh acidity and noticeable tannin. Oak and ageing can add vanilla, spice, leather or savoury flavours, but these are not guaranteed. Australian Tempranillo can range from fresh and elegant to dark, full and spice-driven. Fruit flavours such as cherry and plum do not mean the wine is sweet. Quick answer Tempranillo is a red grape variety from the Iberian Peninsula, best known through Spanish wines. It commonly produces dry red wines with flavours of cherry, plum, blackberry and other red or dark fruits. Fresh acidity gives the wine lift, while tann"},{"title":"What Is Terroir?","description":"Terroir describes how place, climate, site, soil, vine and people interact to shape wine. Learn what the term usefully means.","url":"/what-is-terroir/","text":"Route: /what-is-terroir/ Audience: Australian wine drinkers and beginners Highlights Terroir describes how a placeâ€”and the people working thereâ€”can influence a wineâ€™s character. It includes climate, weather, soil, geology, slope, elevation, water availability, biology and vineyard decisions. Terroir is not simply soil , and wine does not literally taste like the rocks beneath a vineyard. The same grape variety can produce different wines in different places. Vintage, farming and winemaking can make the expression of a site clearer, quieter or quite different. A Geographical Indication identifies where a wine comes from; it does not guarantee one fixed flavour profile. Terroir is a useful way to understand wine, but it is not a mystical guarantee of quality or authenticity. Quick answer: what does terroir mean? In wine, terroir means the combined influence of a particular place and the people working there on the character of a wine . The place may include its climate, seasonal weather, soil, underlying geology, slope, elevation, aspect, drainage, water availability and local biological conditions. The people part includes choices such as grape variety, rootstock, vine spacing, cano"},{"title":"What Is Volatile Acidity in Wine?","description":"Learn what volatile acidity means in wine, how acetic acid and ethyl acetate smell, when VA is acceptable or faulty, and how it differs from ordinary wine acidity.","url":"/what-is-volatile-acidity/","text":"Highlights Volatile acidity, often shortened to VA , refers to volatile acids in wine, mainly acetic acid. Acetic acid can smell like vinegar and taste sharp or sour. A related compound, ethyl acetate, can add nail-polish-remover, glue or solvent-like aromas. VA is different from the normal acidity that gives wine freshness and structure. Small amounts of VA are normal and may add lift or complexity. Excessive or persistent VA can overwhelm fruit and make a wine taste unbalanced. A vinegar or solvent smell is a clue, not proof of a particular fault or compound. Quick answer: is volatile acidity always a fault? No. Volatile acidity is not automatically a fault. In wine science, volatile acidity is an analytical measure of volatile, steam-distillable acids. It is dominated by acetic acid , the compound associated with vinegar-like aroma and sharpness. A related compound called ethyl acetate can add aromas similar to nail-polish remover, acetone, glue or solvent. Small amounts of these compounds can occur naturally during fermentation. They may be barely noticeable, or may contribute a little aromatic lift in some wines. But when volatile acidity becomes strong, persistent or out of b"},{"title":"What Is Whole-Bunch Fermentation?","description":"Whole-bunch fermentation retains some or all grape bunches, including stems, during red-wine fermentation. Learn how it works, how it differs from carbonic maceration, and how it can affect wine.","url":"/winemaking/whole-bunch.html","text":"Highlights Whole-bunch fermentation means fermenting some or all grape bunches with their stems still attached. It is also called whole-cluster fermentation. â€œStem inclusionâ€ is a related term, but can include stems added separately. Stems may change a wineâ€™s tannin, texture, aroma and freshness. The result depends on stem maturity, grape variety, fruit condition, the proportion of whole bunches and cellar decisions. Whole bunch is a winemaking choice, not a guarantee of quality, complexity or a particular flavour. Whole-bunch fermentation is not the same as carbonic maceration. Quick answer Whole-bunch fermentation is a red-winemaking technique in which intact grape bunchesâ€”including the berries, stems and central stalkâ€”are placed in the fermenter. Instead of separating every grape from its stem, the winemaker might retain a small proportion of whole bunches, use a substantial portion, or ferment nearly all the fruit intact. The stems can contribute tannin and alter the wineâ€™s texture. They may also bring herbal, spicy, savoury or floral notes, although these effects are not guaranteed. If the stems are unsuitable or extracted too aggressively, the wine may instead seem green, bit"},{"title":"What Is Wine Aroma? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to Smelling Wine","description":"Learn what wine aroma means, how smell contributes to flavour, where aromas come from and how to identify them with a simple tasting method.","url":"/what-is-wine-aroma/","text":"Highlights Wine aroma is the smell you perceive from wine, both in the glass and while drinking it. Smell contributes greatly to what we commonly call flavour. Taste is only one part of the experience. Wine aromas can be broadly associated with the grape, fermentation, maturation or ageing. Descriptions such as â€œpeachâ€, â€œpepperâ€ or â€œvanillaâ€ are comparisons to familiar smellsâ€”not usually ingredients added to the wine. You do not need to identify precise aromas straight away. Start broad, then become more specific. Aroma intensity and aroma quality are different things: a stronger-smelling wine is not automatically better. Your description does not have to match somebody elseâ€™s. Memory, experience, health and surroundings all influence perception. Quick answer: what is wine aroma? Wine aroma is the smell perceived from wine. You notice it when you smell wine in the glass, and also when you drink it. During drinking, volatile aroma compounds can travel from the mouth towards the nose, contributing to the experience people usually call flavour. This is why a wine can smell one way in the glass and seem different once you taste it. A simple way to explore aroma is to smell the wine bef"},{"title":"What Is Wine Body? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to Light-, Medium- and Full-Bodied Wine","description":"Wine body describes how heavy, full or textured wine feels in your mouth. Learn to recognise light-, medium- and full-bodied wine and avoid common misconceptions.","url":"/what-is-wine-body/","text":"Highlights Wine body is the perceived weight, fullness and texture of wine in your mouth. A light-bodied wine feels relatively delicate; a full-bodied wine feels broader and more substantial. Light, medium and full are useful descriptions, not rigid categories or quality grades. Body is not the same as alcohol, sweetness, acidity, tannin, colour or flavour intensity. The easiest way to assess body is to notice how much presence and texture the wine has across your palate. Grape variety is a clue, not a guarantee: Australian Chardonnay, Shiraz and Pinot Noir can all be made in different styles. Quick Answer: What Does Wine Body Mean? When someone describes a wine as light-bodied , medium-bodied or full-bodied , they are describing how the wine feels in the mouth. A light-bodied wine tends to feel delicate, lean and less substantial. A medium-bodied wine sits between light and full, with moderate weight and fullness. A full-bodied wine generally feels broad, dense, enveloping or more substantial across the palate. â€œWeightâ€ is a sensory comparison, not the physical weight of the wine. Body does not mean that a wine is thick like syrup, either. It is a combined impression created by th"},{"title":"What Is Wine Finish? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to Length and Persistence","description":"Wine finish is what remains after you swallow or spit. Learn how to recognise short, medium and long finishes, describe their character and understand why longer is not always better.","url":"/what-is-wine-finish/","text":"Highlights Wine finish is what remains after you swallow or spit the wine out. It includes lingering flavour, aroma, acidity, sweetness, tannin and mouthfeel. A finish can be short, medium or longâ€”but there are no universal time limits. Finish describes both how long sensations last and what those sensations are like. A long finish is not automatically better than a short one. A simple pause after swallowing or spitting can help you recognise a wineâ€™s finish. Quick answer: what does â€œfinishâ€ mean in wine? Wine finish is the collection of flavours, aromas, tastes and mouthfeel sensations that remain after wine leaves your mouth. You might notice fresh citrus, red fruit, spice, savoury notes, oak, sweetness, tannin, bitterness or alcohol warmth. The finish describes both the character of those sensations and how long they persist. For example, a wine might have a short, clean finish if its flavours fade quickly but neatly. Another might have a long, spicy finish if recognisable spice and fruit remain well after the sip. When wine professionals talk about finish, they may also use the words length , persistence or aftertaste . These terms overlap in everyday wine writing. Finish is of"},{"title":"What Is Wine? A Beginnerâ€™s Guide to How Wine Is Made","description":"Wine is fermented grape juice, but grapes, vineyard conditions, fermentation and winemaking choices create an enormous range of colours, flavours and styles.","url":"/what-is-wine/","text":"Highlights Wine is a beverage made by fermenting grapes or grape juice, usually with yeast. Yeast converts grape sugars into alcohol and carbon dioxide. Wine production is not one fixed recipe. Grape variety, ripeness, climate, site, skin contact and winemaking decisions influence the finished wine. Red, white, rosÃ©, sparkling, sweet and fortified are characteristics or approaches that can overlap. Quick answer Wine is an alcoholic beverage made primarily from grapes or grape juice through alcoholic fermentation. Yeast consumes fermentable grape sugars and produces alcohol and carbon dioxide, along with other compounds that contribute to the wineâ€™s character. The finished wine may then be matured, clarified, stabilised, blended, carbonated, fortified, bottled or packaged, depending on the style. Its colour, aroma, flavour and texture are influenced by the grape variety, ripeness, vineyard conditions, skin contact and decisions made during and after fermentation. On this page From grape to bottle Why wines differ Main types of wine Australian context Terms that need care FAQs From grape to bottle Wine production begins in the vineyard, but the journey from grape to bottle can vary c"},{"title":"When Should Wine Be Decanted?","description":"Learn when decanting can help wine, when it may do little, and how to decant young, old and sediment-bearing bottles safely.","url":"/when-to-decant-wine/","text":"title: &quot;When Should Wine Be Decanted?&quot; description: &quot;Learn when decanting helps wine, when it is unnecessary, and how to decant around sediment without relying on rigid timing rules.&quot; canonical: &quot; https://winedaddy.com.au/when-should-wine-be-decanted/ &quot; article_type: &quot;Educational Guide&quot; When Should Wine Be Decanted? Highlights Decant wine for one of two reasons: to remove sediment or to give some wines more contact with air. Most everyday filtered wines can be served straight from the bottle. Young, powerful reds may become more expressive with air, but this is not guaranteed. Older wines can contain sediment, yet delicate older wines may fade if exposed to too much air. Sediment is generally harmless; it is a natural deposit that can be unpleasant in the glass. Taste the wine first when possible. A small pour can tell you whether decanting is worthwhile. You do not need a special decanter: a clean jug or larger wine glass can sometimes do the job. Quick answer Wine should be decanted when there is a clear purpose. The first purpose is sediment removal . Mature red wines, vintage Port and some minimally filtered or bottle-aged wines can devel"},{"title":"Why Wine Prices Vary â€” And Whether Expensive Wine Is Better","description":"Wine prices reflect grapes, farming, winemaking, packaging, freight, taxes, reputation and demand. Learn how to judge wine value without assuming expensive means better.","url":"/why-wine-prices-vary/","text":"Highlights Wine prices reflect far more than the liquid in the bottle. Grapes, vineyard land, labour, yield, winemaking, packaging, freight, taxes and retail costs can all contribute. Scarcity, reputation and demand can raise a price even when they are not direct production costs. Two wines made from the same grape variety can have very different prices. A higher price may signal extra cost or desirability, but it does not guarantee better quality or greater enjoyment. Value depends on your taste, occasion and budgetâ€”not just the number on the shelf. Quick answer Wine costs more or less because its final price combines production costs with commercial decisions. Vineyard location, grape prices, farming, labour, yield, weather risk, winemaking, maturation, packaging, transport, taxes, distribution and retail margins may all play a part. A wine can also cost more because it is scarce, comes from a highly regarded place, has a strong reputation or is in demand. Those factors may reflect real desirability, but they are not the same as the cost of making the wine. So, does a higher price mean better wine? Not necessarily. Price can provide clues about production, scarcity or reputation,"},{"title":"Wine and Cheese Pairing: How to Choose a Wine for Any Cheese Board","description":"Learn how to pair wine and cheese by matching salt, creaminess, texture, intensity and maturity with wine acidity, sweetness, tannin and body. Includes practical pairings for fresh, soft, washed-rind, hard, aged and blue cheeses.","url":"/wine-and-cheese-pairing/","text":"Highlights Pair wine and cheese by comparing their strongest features, not by following rigid rules. Salt, creaminess, texture, rind, age and flavour intensity all affect the match. Fresh cheeses usually suit fresh, lighter wines; creamy cheeses often enjoy acidity or bubbles. Sweet or fortified wines are excellent starting points for salty, intense blue cheese. Tannic reds can be powerful with delicate or salty cheeses, so choose them carefully. A dry wine can still taste fruity. Dryness describes sweetness, not the absence of fruit flavour. There is no single perfect wine for every cheese. The best pairing is the one that tastes balanced and enjoyable to you. Quick answer: how do you pair wine and cheese? Start by identifying the cheeseâ€™s main character. Is it fresh and tangy, creamy, pungent, firm, salty or intensely savoury? Then choose a wine that either matches that intensity or provides a useful contrast. Fresh, mild cheeses generally work with crisp whites such as Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc or Pinot Gris, as well as fresh sparkling wine. Creamy cheeses often benefit from acidity and bubbles, which create freshness between bites. Firm or aged cheeses can support wines with mo"},{"title":"Wine and Seafood Pairing: What Wine Goes Best With Seafood?","description":"Learn how to pair wine with oysters, fish, prawns, shellfish, salmon, fried seafood and spicy seafood by considering preparation, sauce, richness and texture.","url":"/wine-and-seafood-pairing/","text":"Highlights There is no single best wine for all seafood. Match the whole dish: seafood, cooking method, sauce, seasoning and richness. Fresh white wines and sparkling wine are reliable starting points for delicate, raw or fried seafood. Fuller Chardonnay, rosÃ© and selected light reds can suit grilled, smoked or oily seafood. A little sweetness can work well with chilli-forward seafood. Sauce and preparation can matter more than the seafood species. Pairing suggestions are starting points, not rules. Quick answer The best wine for seafood depends on how the seafood is prepared. For raw oysters, delicate white fish and lightly seasoned shellfish, start with a fresh, high-acid wine such as Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris or sparkling wine. Fried seafood often works with sparkling wine or a crisp white because freshness and bubbles provide contrast to oil, salt and crunch. Richer dishes need a different approach. Grilled or roasted fish may suit textured Chardonnay, dry rosÃ© or a light Pinot Noir. Salmon and other oily fish can support Chardonnay, Riesling, rosÃ©, sparkling rosÃ© or Pinot Noir, depending on the sauce and cooking method. For chilli-forward seafood, try a fresh wine "},{"title":"Wine and Spicy Food Pairing: How to Choose the Right Wine","description":"Learn how to pair wine with chilli-hot food by matching sweetness, acidity, alcohol, tannin, body and bubbles to the complete dish.","url":"/wine-and-spicy-food-pairing/","text":"Highlights There is no single best wine for every spicy dish. With strong chilli heat, fresh, fruit-driven, moderate-alcohol wines are often comfortable starting points. A little sweetness can soften the impression of heat, but it does not remove or â€œneutraliseâ€ capsaicin. Acidity refreshes rich, creamy and coconut-based dishes. High alcohol, firm tannin and heavy oak can make chilli dishes feel hotter or harsher. Slightly off-dry Riesling is a useful first choice, but sparkling wine, rosÃ©, aromatic whites and selected reds can also work. Pair the whole dishâ€”not just the chilli levelâ€”with the wine. Quick answer: what wine goes best with spicy food? A slightly off-dry Riesling is one of the most useful starting points for chilli-hot food. Its freshness, fruit character and gentle sweetness can balance heat without making the wine feel heavy. But the best choice depends on the dish. For fresh chilli and herbs, try Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc or another aromatic white. For salty or fried spicy food, sparkling wine can provide refreshing acidity and bubbles. RosÃ© suits many grilled or moderately spicy dishes. A fruit-driven red with moderate tannin can work with smoky, grilled or tomato-"},{"title":"Wine and Steak Pairing: What Wine Goes Best with Steak?","description":"Learn how to pair wine with steak by considering the cut, cooking method, char, sauce and richness. Includes Australian Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir and alternatives.","url":"/wine-and-steak-pairing/","text":"There is no single best wine for every steak. The most reliable approach is to match the wine to the whole dish : the cut, its richness, how it was cooked, the amount of char or smoke, and the sauce. As a starting point, rich or heavily grilled steaks can suit structured reds such as Australian Shiraz or Cabernet Sauvignon. Leaner, more delicately flavoured cuts may be better with a fresher, softer red such as Pinot Noir. But sauce and seasoning can change the answer just as much as the steak itself. Highlights Rich, fatty steak can work well with a wine that has body, acidity and tannin. Tannin is the drying, grippy sensation found in many red wines. Acidity adds freshness and can stop a rich dish feeling heavy. Char, smoke, pepper, chilli and sweet barbecue sauce all affect the pairing. Australian Shiraz and Cabernet Sauvignon come in many styles; neither is always the right choice. Lean steak can suit moderate-bodied red wine, fuller Chardonnay, rosÃ© or sparkling wine. Personal preference is part of a successful pairing. The quick answer: which wine should you choose? Start by asking three questions: Is the steak rich or lean? Is the dominant flavour beef, char, smoke or sauce? "},{"title":"Wine Glass Types Explained: Which Glass Should You Use?","description":"Learn how wine-glass shape affects aroma and drinking experience, and choose practical glassware for red, white, sparkling, sweet and fortified wine.","url":"/wine-glass-types-explained/","text":"Highlights Glass shape can influence how wine smells and feels to drink, but it does not transform the wine. For most homes, one clean, comfortable all-purpose glass is enough. A wider bowl can give aromas more room; a narrower rim can help concentrate them near your nose. Red, white, sparkling and dessert-wine glasses are useful categories, not strict rules. Stemmed glasses help reduce hand contact with the bowl; stemless glasses can be more stable and convenient. Cleanliness, comfort, storage and breakage risk matter more than owning expensive specialist glassware. Quick answer: does wine glass shape really matter? Yes, but usually in a subtle way. Wine-glass shape can influence how aromas gather above the wine and how you encounter them when you smell and sip. Sensory studies have found differences in aroma perception between wines served in differently shaped glasses. However, the effect varies with the wine, the glass and the drinker. A specialist glass will not rescue a poor wine or turn an ordinary bottle into something extraordinary. For most people, the best starting point is a clean, comfortable glass with a bowl that is wider than its rim. It should leave enough room for"},{"title":"Wine Serving Temperature Explained","description":"Learn practical serving temperatures for red, white, sparkling and fortified wine, and how temperature changes aroma, flavour and texture.","url":"/wine-serving-temperature-explained/","text":"Front matter Title: Wine Serving Temperature Explained: The Right Temperature for Every Style Primary entity: Wine serving temperature Audience: Australian wine drinkers, hosts and beginners Wine Serving Temperature Explained: The Right Temperature for Every Style Highlights Wine is best served within a useful range, not at one magical temperature. Lighter, more delicate wines generally suit cooler service. Fuller-bodied and more structured wines usually show better slightly warmer. Light-bodied red wine can benefit from a short chill. â€œRoom temperatureâ€ is not a reliable rule, especially in a warm Australian home. Too much cold can mute aroma and flavour; too much warmth can make wine seem heavy or alcoholic. A normal refrigerator and an ice-and-water bucket are usually all you need. Quick answer The right serving temperature depends more on a wineâ€™s body and structure than simply whether it is red or white. As a practical starting point, serve sparkling wine at 5â€“10Â°C , light- to medium-bodied white wine at 7â€“12Â°C , full-bodied white wine at 10â€“13Â°C , rosÃ© at 7â€“14Â°C , light-bodied red wine at 12â€“17Â°C , and medium- to full-bodied red wine at 16â€“20Â°C . Sweet and fortified wines var"},{"title":"Wrattonbully wine region: a guide to the Limestone Coast GI","description":"Understand Wrattonbully wine region, its South Australian GI identity, Limestone Coast location, climate, soils, key grape varieties and differences from Coonawarra and Padthaway.","url":"/wrattonbully-wine-region/","text":"Highlights Wrattonbully is a protected wine region, or Geographical Indication (GI), in south-eastern South Australia. It sits within the broader Limestone Coast wine zone, between the separate GIs of Padthaway and Coonawarra. The region is associated with rolling terrain, elevated vineyard sites and red terra rossa soils over limestone. Cabernet Sauvignon is Wrattonbullyâ€™s dominant variety, with Shiraz, Chardonnay and Merlot also important. Wrattonbully wines can be structured and fruit-driven, but the GI does not guarantee one flavour profile or quality level. â€œWrattonbullyâ€ on a label tells you about geographic origin. You still need the variety, vintage, producer and winemaking details to understand the wine fully. Quick answer: what is Wrattonbully wine region? Wrattonbully is a protected South Australian wine GI within the Limestone Coast wine zone. It is near the Naracoorte Ranges and the Victorian border, with Naracoorte serving as a useful practical centre for orientation. The region lies between the separate Limestone Coast GIs of Padthaway to the north and Coonawarra to the south. For Australian travellers, Wrattonbully is approximately a four-hour drive south-east of Ad"},{"title":"Yarra Valley Wine Region: Wines, Climate and What to Expect","description":"Explore the Yarra Valleyâ€™s varied climate, elevations and key wines, including Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon and sparkling wine.","url":"/yarra-valley-wine-region/","text":"The Yarra Valley is a recognised Victorian wine region and Australian Geographical Indication just east of Melbourne. It is particularly known for cool-climate Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, including sparkling wine. Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz and Pinot Gris/Grigio are also important. The region is not defined by one fixed flavour. Elevation, aspect, rainfall, soils, vintage, grape variety, producer and winemaking all influence the finished wine. That is why a Yarra Valley Pinot Noir can feel quite different from a Yarra Valley Chardonnayâ€”or even from another Pinot Noir made elsewhere in the region. Highlights The Yarra Valley is in Victoria, just east of Melbourne, within the broader Port Phillip GI. It is one of Australiaâ€™s historic wine regions, with vines first planted at Yering in 1838. Pinot Noir and Chardonnay are the regionâ€™s best-known varieties. Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz and Pinot Gris/Grigio add considerable variety. Cool conditions can support freshness, aromatic detail and slower ripening. Elevation, aspect, rainfall and soils vary widely across the GI. Lower Yarra and Upper Yarra are useful geographic descriptions, not automatically separate legal GIs. â€œYarra Valleyâ€ on "}]
